qrt{3}),$ find the polar equation of the ray. Assume that the polar angle is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the reference angle associated with the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-5\\sqrt{3}}{-5}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the ray lies in the third quadrant. So, we find the angle in the third quadrant that has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-274767/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d90b9572-cdac-51f7-b386-81b750642585"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-274767"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-41263"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/assets/34b473c8e212297d8cb434fe0a2dd9872cfa2fda924ac3a4d528bc1318af8c5f.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=8$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-120725/selection/3774ea19c2edbbdb4fd4074c0f30d826eb7ce61f6a6298374b9332a19a64dca1" :answer/id #uuid "77edfa21-9e9f-5abb-9b50-8ca611839d7c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3\\sqrt{3},-3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120725/selection/c794bd91fafb22a9d44d418b426dc48e1f5ff8baeb0fa9196ef9f62ad2b94178" :answer/id #uuid "568d2bb1-4f45-517d-ab45-68ee966db7c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3\\sqrt{2},-3\\sqrt{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120725/selection/77396a87a15fca0a1e9c872ae686d64ac94a2667bbe1730f7de9858864432014" :answer/id #uuid "3b4012e0-c379-5ac2-91b4-f8ca77534215" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-3\\sqrt{3},3\\sqrt{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120725/selection/582475f91bc539ff144750f54fdc98baf4312493db28d2f14a807de0b961cb67" :answer/id #uuid "41fd0d7f-89bc-5171-846b-81958af77d2a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3\\sqrt{3},-3\\sqrt{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120725/selection/c0b52b408c35f15482d5042401f3645ec1799e206e9a05f23661ebe2d0daac50" :answer/id #uuid "2e3a311e-a831-5d22-8734-c423e776099f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-3,3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120725/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5ef17ec4-4afd-5919-90a6-4f63cd444f4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-120725/selection/3774ea19c2edbbdb4fd4074c0f30d826eb7ce61f6a6298374b9332a19a64dca1" :answer-field/choices ["q-120725/selection/3774ea19c2edbbdb4fd4074c0f30d826eb7ce61f6a6298374b9332a19a64dca1" "q-120725/selection/c794bd91fafb22a9d44d418b426dc48e1f5ff8baeb0fa9196ef9f62ad2b94178" "q-120725/selection/77396a87a15fca0a1e9c872ae686d64ac94a2667bbe1730f7de9858864432014" "q-120725/selection/582475f91bc539ff144750f54fdc98baf4312493db28d2f14a807de0b961cb67" "q-120725/selection/c0b52b408c35f15482d5042401f3645ec1799e206e9a05f23661ebe2d0daac50"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-120725" :question/id #uuid "fffb59d3-cca6-5381-9439-7548b7d65a74" :question/math-academy-id "q-120725" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/assets/d8bbcd878edaef5d3f5b92d106a071444f20c72f3d419689d15644a4842a2d45.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=6$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r,\\theta )=(6,\\frac{11\\pi }{6}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(3\\sqrt{3},-3).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-120725/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc8d4944-9005-5712-ad88-744dac83603e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-120725"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-120719"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/assets/3b1318b156281c7bc8724f988545d9f5c9fab2bda4ed88c387f051dbe5bece1e.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=12$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-87139"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/assets/386a4b395d45276284606b0f079be16881af717ba7e791ab62d8df34743f0269.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=6$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-321972/field-1/f853eeffac47c628af4a2b7e7b0ecd024652f548b0b0007bc18dba21ad82ad7c" :answer/id #uuid "23437580-af33-5532-8b28-12b8403481dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-321972/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "60832ce1-9940-558e-bb8a-ac1fbd44495f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-321972/field-1/f853eeffac47c628af4a2b7e7b0ecd024652f548b0b0007bc18dba21ad82ad7c" :answer-field/choices ["q-321972/field-1/f853eeffac47c628af4a2b7e7b0ecd024652f548b0b0007bc18dba21ad82ad7c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-321972/field-2/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "8244dc1f-5f7f-53f3-82e9-825735b1aac3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-321972/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "590fa078-5e18-5f15-917a-7849ff5b53bf" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-321972/field-2/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer-field/choices ["q-321972/field-2/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-321972" :question/id #uuid "bcaa98ac-5620-5ad5-b3d4-f9e5ed073000" :question/math-academy-id "q-321972" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/assets/6aeb571c312e4dcc0f9ffc4fe2ad9b1a1db5cf02a2f757b66191e31337a8c498.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=6\\sqrt{2}$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. The Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r,\\theta )=(6\\sqrt{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}\\,cos\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-6 \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}\\,sin\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-6,6).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-321972/field-1" "q-321972/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc8d4944-9005-5712-ad88-744dac83603e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-321972"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5389"] :question/worked-solution "The polar curve defined by the equation $\\theta=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$ is the ray that starts at the origin and makes a counterclockwise angle of $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nThe correct graph is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/assets/8c25245d58ec9ddd8d8f1e73f8eae12c244ecd32018f939f1aba86b274622ba8.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5390"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/assets/245f03c8eeb5b84f7b4caafd61f37b7c15e77e77a447fc762170e05d61c87864.png)\n\nWhat is the polar equation of the ray shown above?" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5391"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/assets/1e1362c20f9e20bb22d049d8b218d786a5254603ab4cc36d90b5866095a8897c.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(6,-2\\sqrt{3})$, find the polar equation of the ray." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5392"] :question/problem "The polar curve $r=7$ and the ray $\\theta=\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Aca